<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rajputana</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rajputana was the historical name of the region of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> roughly corresponding to present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, so called by the British after its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> ruling dynasties. Under British rule it consisted of some two dozen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, including Mewar, Marwar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a> and Bikaner, supervised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputana-agency">Rajputana Agency</a>, together with the small province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer-merwara">Ajmer-Merwara</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rajputana.jpg" alt="Rajputana" width="320" height="266" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Tom Radulovich at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rajputana_1909.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
